Esc then a key as Alt-key at the shell prompt. read_key joins ESC to the key after it only when
that key arrives within 50 ms, which is how a terminal sends Alt (Option as Meta) as one burst.
Typed by hand, as Mac users do (Esc, then Backspace), the pause is longer: the ESC was dropped
and Backspace removed one character instead of a word. readline and zle treat ESC as the Meta
prefix with no time limit; lineedit now does too, for Backspace, d, f and b (the Alt keys it
binds) and '.'. Any other key after ESC is taken as itself.

Alt-. (Esc .) is new, as in bash: it inserts the last word of the previous command (quotes kept,
a quoted space does not split it); pressed again, that word gives way to the one from the
command before. read_key does not map ESC '.', so busybox vi's "Esc ." stays two keys.

diff --git a/libbb/lineedit.c b/libbb/lineedit.c
index 151208c..539ad19 100644
--- a/libbb/lineedit.c
+++ b/libbb/lineedit.c
@@ -1861,6 +1861,135 @@ static void ctrl_right(void)
